;_&gt;_*]:min-w-0 whitespace-normal break-words print:block"><p dir=3D"ltr"=
>The easiest way to read these three diagrams is as <strong>three snapshots=
 of the same game</strong>. Stage 1 is the skeleton. Stage 2 and Stage 3 ke=
ep almost all of it and attach new parts at a few specific points. Once the=
 Stage 1 skeleton makes sense, the later diagrams are mostly =E2=80=9Cthe s=
ame thing plus a few new boxes.=E2=80=9D</p>
<h3 dir=3D"ltr" aria-level=3D"4">1. How to read the diagrams</h3>
<ul dir=3D"ltr">
<li><strong>Colours (the BCE pattern from your slides):</strong>
<ul dir=3D"ltr">
<li>Green =3D <strong>boundary</strong>: talks to the outside world (<code =
dir=3D"ltr">Main</code>, <code dir=3D"ltr">ConsoleUI</code>, <code dir=3D"l=
tr">GameView</code>).</li>
<li>Blue =3D <strong>control</strong>: runs the rules and makes things happ=
en (<code dir=3D"ltr">GameController</code>, <code dir=3D"ltr">Waiter</code=
>, <code dir=3D"ltr">Chef</code>, <code dir=3D"ltr">Cashier</code>, <code d=
ir=3D"ltr">Host</code>).</li>
<li>Yellow =3D <strong>entity</strong>: holds the game=E2=80=99s data and s=
tate (<code dir=3D"ltr">Customer</code>, <code dir=3D"ltr">Order</code>, <c=
ode dir=3D"ltr">Table</code>, <code dir=3D"ltr">MenuItem</code>, <code dir=
=3D"ltr">GameStats</code>).</li>
<li>Purple =3D <strong>enum</strong>: a fixed set of states.</li>
</ul>
</li>
<li><strong>Arrows:</strong>
<ul dir=3D"ltr">
<li>Filled diamond means the owner creates and owns the part. For example, =
<code dir=3D"ltr">GameController</code> =E2=97=86=E2=86=92 <code dir=3D"ltr=
">Waiter</code>.</li>
<li>Solid arrow is a stored field, such as <code dir=3D"ltr">Order =E2=86=
=92 Customer</code> (=E2=80=9Cevery order remembers its customer=E2=80=9D).=
</li>
<li>Dashed arrow means =E2=80=9Cuses it temporarily.=E2=80=9D For example, =
<code dir=3D"ltr">Chef</code> is given an <code dir=3D"ltr">Order</code> as=
 a parameter but doesn=E2=80=99t keep it.</li>
<li>Hollow triangle is inheritance (solid line) or implements (dashed line)=
.</li>
</ul>
</li>
</ul>
<h3 dir=3D"ltr" aria-level=3D"4">2. Stage 1: the skeleton</h3>
<p dir=3D"ltr">The best way to understand it is to follow one turn through =
the classes:</p>
<ol dir=3D"ltr">
<li><strong><code dir=3D"ltr">Main</code></strong> creates a <code dir=3D"l=
tr">ConsoleUI</code>, an <code dir=3D"ltr">ArrivalSchedule</code>, the <cod=
e dir=3D"ltr">Menu</code> and a <code dir=3D"ltr">GameController</code>, th=
en calls <code dir=3D"ltr">play()</code>.</li>
<li><strong><code dir=3D"ltr">GameController</code></strong> is the centre =
of everything. It owns the staff (<code dir=3D"ltr">Waiter</code>, <code di=
r=3D"ltr">Chef</code>, <code dir=3D"ltr">Cashier</code>), the <code dir=3D"=
ltr">GameStats</code>, the tables, the <code dir=3D"ltr">WaitingQueue</code=
>, and the lists of customers and orders. Its private methods (<code dir=3D=
"ltr">arrivals()</code>, <code dir=3D"ltr">waiterPhase()</code>, <code dir=
=3D"ltr">chefPhase()</code>, <code dir=3D"ltr">paymentAndEating()</code>, <=
code dir=3D"ltr">satisfactionUpdate()</code>) are the six turn steps from s=
ection 3.2 of the brief, one method per step.</li>
<li><strong>Arrivals:</strong> <code dir=3D"ltr">ArrivalSchedule.arrivalsOn=
(turn)</code> says how many customers appear. The controller creates each <=
code dir=3D"ltr">Customer</code> and puts it in the <code dir=3D"ltr">Waiti=
ngQueue</code>.</li>
<li><strong>Waiter phase:</strong> the controller asks the player through <=
code dir=3D"ltr">GameView.choose(...)</code>, then calls one of <code dir=
=3D"ltr">Waiter.seat(...)</code>, <code dir=3D"ltr">takeOrder(...)</code> o=
r <code dir=3D"ltr">serve(...)</code>. When the Waiter takes an order, the =
<code dir=3D"ltr">Customer</code> picks its own dish with <code dir=3D"ltr"=
>chooseFood(menu)</code> and an <code dir=3D"ltr">Order</code> is created w=
ith <code dir=3D"ltr">Order.createOrder(...)</code>.</li>
<li><strong>Chef phase:</strong> <code dir=3D"ltr">Chef.work(order)</code> =
calls <code dir=3D"ltr">order.prepareOneUnit()</code>. When <code dir=3D"lt=
r">unitsDone</code> reaches the dish=E2=80=99s <code dir=3D"ltr">prepUnits<=
/code>, the order becomes <code dir=3D"ltr">READY</code>.</li>
<li><strong>Payment:</strong> <code dir=3D"ltr">Cashier</code> keeps a <cod=
e dir=3D"ltr">paymentLine</code> of orders. <code dir=3D"ltr">collect(...)<=
/code> charges the oldest one, records it in <code dir=3D"ltr">GameStats.re=
cordPayment(...)</code>, and frees the <code dir=3D"ltr">Table</code>.</li>
<li><strong>Satisfaction:</strong> waiting or seated customers lose 8 point=
s (<code dir=3D"ltr">LOSS_PER_TURN =3D 8</code>). If a customer reaches 0, =
<code dir=3D"ltr">handleDeparture()</code> cancels the order and frees the =
table.</li>
<li><strong>Result:</strong> after 18 turns, <code dir=3D"ltr">GameStats.is=
Victory()</code> checks the targets in <code dir=3D"ltr">GameConfig</code>.=
</li>
</ol>
<p dir=3D"ltr">The two enums are the =E2=80=9Ctraffic lights=E2=80=9D: <cod=
e dir=3D"ltr">CustomerState</code> (WAITING =E2=86=92 SEATED =E2=86=92 ORDE=
RED =E2=86=92 EATING =E2=86=92 READY_TO_PAY =E2=86=92 PAID/LEFT) and <code =
dir=3D"ltr">OrderStatus</code> (PLACED =E2=86=92 READY =E2=86=92 SERVED =E2=
=86=92 PAID/CANCELLED). This ties to the Encapsulation note in your diagram=
: no class can set a state directly. A state changes only through methods l=
ike <code dir=3D"ltr">serve()</code> and <code dir=3D"ltr">markPaid()</code=
>, and those check the current state first with <code dir=3D"ltr">requireSt=
ate</code>.</p>
<p dir=3D"ltr">The <code dir=3D"ltr">GameView</code> interface is the DIP n=
ote. The controller only knows =E2=80=9Csome view,=E2=80=9D so tests can pl=
ug in a fake scripted view instead of the real console.</p>
<h3 dir=3D"ltr" aria-level=3D"4">3. Stage 1 =E2=86=92 Stage 2: customer typ=
es</h3>
<p dir=3D"ltr">The brief asks for VIP and Critic customers <strong>without =
type checks in the controller</strong>. So the one real change is to <stron=
g><code dir=3D"ltr">Customer</code></strong>:</p>
<div dir=3D"ltr" class=3D"md-table-scroll prose-scroll print:overflow-x-vis=
ible"><table><thead><tr><th scope=3D"col">Stage 1</th><th scope=3D"col">Sta=
ge 2</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code dir=3D"ltr">Customer</code> is a=
 normal class with <code dir=3D"ltr">LOSS_PER_TURN =3D 8</code></td><td><co=
de dir=3D"ltr">Customer</code> becomes <strong>abstract</strong></td></tr><=
tr><td>=E2=80=94</td><td><code dir=3D"ltr">RegularCustomer</code> (8), <cod=
e dir=3D"ltr">VIPCustomer</code> (5, 10% off) and <code dir=3D"ltr">CriticC=
ustomer</code> (12, orders the most expensive dish) inherit from it</td></t=
r></tbody></table></div>
<p dir=3D"ltr">How the polymorphism works:</p>
<ul dir=3D"ltr">
<li><code dir=3D"ltr">Customer</code> keeps the shared flow and marks those=
 methods <code dir=3D"ltr">{leaf}</code> (final): <code dir=3D"ltr">seat()<=
/code>, <code dir=3D"ltr">serve()</code>, <code dir=3D"ltr">pay()</code> an=
d so on. No subclass can change the order of events.</li>
<li>The subclasses only override small <strong>hooks</strong>: <code dir=3D=
"ltr">satisfactionLossPerTurn()</code>, <code dir=3D"ltr">getDiscountPercen=
t()</code> and <code dir=3D"ltr">pickDish(...)</code>. This is the template=
 method pattern.</li>
<li><code dir=3D"ltr">GameController</code> still just calls <code dir=3D"l=
tr">customer.loseSatisfaction()</code>. It never asks =E2=80=9Care you a VI=
P?=E2=80=9D The object answers for itself.</li>
</ul>
<p dir=3D"ltr">Smaller changes that go with this:</p>
<ul dir=3D"ltr">
<li><code dir=3D"ltr">ArrivalSchedule</code> now holds a list of actual <co=
de dir=3D"ltr">Customer</code> objects instead of just turn numbers. That=
=E2=80=99s how one VIP and one Critic get into the six arrivals.</li>
<li><code dir=3D"ltr">GameConfig.baseCustomers()</code> builds that list.</=
li>
<li><code dir=3D"ltr">Money</code> gains <code dir=3D"ltr">percentOff(...)<=
/code> for the VIP discount.</li>
</ul>
<p dir=3D"ltr">In Stage 2 the layout is redrawn and tables, queue and order=
s become proper arrows instead of attribute lines. That=E2=80=99s why the d=
iagram looks so different even though the structure is nearly the same.</p>
<h3 dir=3D"ltr" aria-level=3D"4">4. Stage 2 =E2=86=92 Stage 3: Host, ComboM=
eal, Happy Hour</h3>
<p dir=3D"ltr">There are three new features, and each plugs into one spot:<=
/p>
<ul dir=3D"ltr">
<li><strong><code dir=3D"ltr">Host</code></strong> is a new control class o=
wned by <code dir=3D"ltr">GameController</code>, with one method: <code dir=
=3D"ltr">seatFirstInQueue(queue, tables)</code>. The controller gets a <cod=
e dir=3D"ltr">hostPhase()</code> and a <code dir=3D"ltr">hostEnabled</code>=
 flag, so Stage 1 and 2 behaviour can still be run.</li>
<li><strong><code dir=3D"ltr">ComboMeal</code></strong> <strong>extends <co=
de dir=3D"ltr">MenuItem</code></strong> and <strong>holds two <code dir=3D"=
ltr">MenuItem</code>s</strong> (the hollow diamond is aggregation, labelled=
 =E2=80=9Cfirst, second=E2=80=9D). Because a combo <em>is</em> a <code dir=
=3D"ltr">MenuItem</code>, <code dir=3D"ltr">Order</code>, <code dir=3D"ltr"=
>Chef</code> and <code dir=3D"ltr">Waiter</code> didn=E2=80=99t need to cha=
nge at all; they treat it like any dish. That=E2=80=99s the Polymorphism an=
d composition (Stage 3) note. <code dir=3D"ltr">Menu.withCombos()</code> bu=
ilds the menu that includes combos.</li>
<li><strong><code dir=3D"ltr">HappyHour</code></strong> is a new entity tha=
t tracks <code dir=3D"ltr">used</code> and <code dir=3D"ltr">turnsLeft</cod=
e>, with <code dir=3D"ltr">applyTo(price)</code> and <code dir=3D"ltr">reco=
verWaiting(customers)</code>. Two things change because of it:
<ul dir=3D"ltr">
<li><code dir=3D"ltr">Order</code> now stores a <strong><code dir=3D"ltr">p=
rice</code></strong> fixed at the moment it=E2=80=99s taken (VIP discount f=
irst, then Happy Hour), so later changes don=E2=80=99t affect it. That=E2=
=80=99s why <code dir=3D"ltr">createOrder</code> and <code dir=3D"ltr">Wait=
er.takeOrder</code> have a new overload with a <code dir=3D"ltr">happyHour<=
/code> parameter.</li>
<li>In <code dir=3D"ltr">recoverWaiting</code>, Happy Hour only says =E2=80=
=9C+15=E2=80=9D. <code dir=3D"ltr">Customer.recover()</code> decides whethe=
r it=E2=80=99s allowed (only WAITING/SEATED/ORDERED, capped at 100). That=
=E2=80=99s encapsulation again.</li>
</ul>
</li>
</ul>
<p dir=3D"ltr"><code dir=3D"ltr">GameConfig</code> also gains scenario swit=
ches (<code dir=3D"ltr">STAGE_1/2/3</code>, <code dir=3D"ltr">hostEnabled(.=
